I've characterized Quicksilver's two build variants with evidence-backed scheduling shapes:

## Summary

**Subject**: `qs` — Quicksilver (LLNL), a single executable built from `src/Makefile`, a Monte Carlo particle-transport proxy app.

**Variant 1: `cpu-mpi-openmp`** (default/typical build)
- MPI ranks arranged in a 3D domain decomposition (I×J×K), each rank optionally multithreaded via OpenMP (`OMP_NUM_THREADS`)
- Communication: neighbor-exchange of particles crossing domain boundaries each cycle (`MPI_Isend`/`Irecv` via `SendQueue`/`MC_Particle_Buffer`), plus periodic small `MPI_Allreduce`/`Iallreduce` for tallies and a distributed termination test (`Test_Done_New`)
- NUMA/affinity-aware: example SLURM/aprun scripts explicitly set `OMP_PROC_BIND`, `OMP_PLACES=cores`, `--mpibind`, `-cc depth`
- No shared-memory windows, no checkpoint/restart, no parallel I/O library
- Scales from 1 to 24,576 nodes in CORAL2 benchmark scripts (1 rank/node, 64 threads/rank), so topology span is flexible (single-node to many-node)

**Variant 2: `gpu-cuda-hip`** (built with `-DHAVE_CUDA`/`-DHAVE_HIP`/`GPU_NATIVE`)
- Same MPI domain-decomposition and neighbor-exchange communication pattern as CPU build
- Adds GPU offload of the core `CycleTrackingKernel`; one GPU bound per MPI rank via `GPUID = rank % Ngpus; gpuSetDevice(GPUID)` — no evidence of multi-GPU-per-rank or NCCL/RCCL/GPU-aware MPI (particle exchange stays host-side)
- Requires rank↔GPU co-location; otherwise same neighbor placement and lack of checkpoint/parallel-I/O as CPU build

Both traces recorded with evidence citations; GPU variant confidence is medium since GPU-communication specifics (peer-to-peer, GPU-aware MPI) weren't found explicitly in source.
